// Zenyo class notes: high school biology. Written for Zenyo by apisesfr's project (original text, free to use and sell).
// Each note: t = title, k = extra search words, x = the note. The site finds the best 1-2 notes for a question and hands them to Zenyo.
window.LUMO_NOTES = (window.LUMO_NOTES || []).concat([
// ---------- science skills & life ----------
{ t: "Characteristics of life", k: "living things alive nonliving traits of life", x: "All living things: are made of cells, use energy (metabolism), grow and develop, reproduce, respond to their environment (stimuli), keep a stable inside (homeostasis), have DNA, and as groups evolve over time. Viruses are not usually called living because they are not cells and can only reproduce inside a host cell." },
{ t: "Scientific method and variables", k: "experiment hypothesis independent dependent control variable constant controlled group", x: "Scientific method: ask a question, make a hypothesis (a testable prediction, often 'If... then...'), test it with an experiment, collect data, and draw a conclusion. Independent variable = the one thing you change on purpose. Dependent variable = what you measure (it depends on the independent one). Controlled variables (constants) = things kept the same. Control group = the group that gets no treatment, used for comparison. Repeat trials to make results reliable." },
{ t: "Microscopes", k: "microscope magnification objective eyepiece lens compound electron total", x: "A compound light microscope uses lenses and light. Total magnification = eyepiece power x objective power (10x eyepiece with 40x objective = 400x). Start on the lowest objective, focus with the coarse knob, then use only the fine knob on high power. Electron microscopes magnify far more and can show tiny parts like ribosomes, but they cannot view living cells." },
// ---------- cells ----------
{ t: "Cell theory", k: "cell theory schleiden schwann virchow hooke basic unit", x: "Cell theory: 1) all living things are made of one or more cells, 2) the cell is the basic unit of structure and function in living things, 3) all cells come from pre-existing cells. Robert Hooke named cells (cork, 1665); Schleiden (plants), Schwann (animals) and Virchow (cells come from cells) built the theory." },
{ t: "Prokaryotic vs eukaryotic cells", k: "prokaryote eukaryote bacteria nucleus difference organelles", x: "Prokaryotic cells (bacteria and archaea) have NO nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles; their DNA is a loop in the cytoplasm (nucleoid). They are small and simple but do have ribosomes, a cell membrane, cytoplasm and usually a cell wall. Eukaryotic cells (plants, animals, fungi, protists) HAVE a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles like mitochondria, and are usually much bigger. Tip: 'pro = no' nucleus, 'eu = do'." },
{ t: "Plant cells vs animal cells", k: "plant animal cell difference cell wall chloroplast vacuole compare", x: "Both plant and animal cells have a nucleus, cell membrane, cytoplasm, mitochondria, ribosomes, ER and Golgi. Only plant cells have a cell wall (cellulose, gives a box shape), chloroplasts (photosynthesis) and one large central vacuole (stores water, keeps the cell firm). Animal cells have small vacuoles, lysosomes, centrioles, and a rounder shape." },
{ t: "Nucleus", k: "nucleus nucleolus nuclear envelope control center chromatin", x: "The nucleus is the control center of a eukaryotic cell. It holds the cell's DNA (as chromatin, which coils into chromosomes when the cell divides). It is surrounded by a double membrane called the nuclear envelope with pores that let RNA out. The nucleolus inside it makes ribosomes." },
{ t: "Mitochondria", k: "mitochondria mitochondrion powerhouse organelle makes energy atp cellular respiration", x: "Mitochondria are the 'powerhouse of the cell'. They do cellular respiration: they break down glucose using oxygen to make ATP, the energy molecule cells use. Both plant and animal cells have them. Cells that use lots of energy (like muscle cells) have many. Mitochondria have their own DNA and a folded inner membrane (cristae)." },
{ t: "Chloroplasts", k: "chloroplast chlorophyll green pigment photosynthesis thylakoid stroma leaves change color fall autumn", x: "Chloroplasts are organelles in plant and algae cells where photosynthesis happens. They contain chlorophyll, a green pigment that absorbs light energy (mostly red and blue light; green is reflected, which is why plants look green). Inside are stacks of thylakoids (light reactions) surrounded by fluid called stroma (Calvin cycle). Animal cells do not have chloroplasts." },
{ t: "Ribosomes", k: "ribosome protein synthesis make proteins rrna", x: "Ribosomes make proteins. They read mRNA and join amino acids together in the right order (translation). They are made of rRNA and protein and are found in ALL cells, prokaryotes and eukaryotes. They float free in the cytoplasm or sit on the rough ER." },
{ t: "Endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi", k: "endoplasmic reticulum rough smooth er golgi apparatus body package ship", x: "Rough ER is covered in ribosomes and makes and folds proteins. Smooth ER has no ribosomes; it makes lipids and breaks down toxins. The Golgi apparatus (Golgi body) receives proteins from the ER, modifies, sorts and packages them into vesicles, and ships them to where they are needed, like a post office." },
{ t: "Lysosomes, vacuoles, vesicles and cytoskeleton", k: "lysosome vacuole vesicle centriole cytoskeleton cytoplasm digest", x: "Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes that break down old cell parts, food and invaders (mostly in animal cells). Vacuoles store water, food and waste; plants have one big central vacuole. Vesicles are small transport bubbles. Cytoplasm is the jelly-like fluid that fills the cell. The cytoskeleton (protein fibers) gives the cell shape and helps it move. Centrioles help organize cell division in animal cells." },
{ t: "Cell wall", k: "cell wall cellulose chitin peptidoglycan support", x: "The cell wall is a stiff layer outside the cell membrane that supports and protects the cell. Plant cell walls are made of cellulose, fungus cell walls of chitin, and bacteria cell walls of peptidoglycan. Animal cells have no cell wall." },
{ t: "Cell membrane", k: "cell membrane plasma membrane phospholipid bilayer fluid mosaic selectively permeable", x: "The cell membrane controls what enters and leaves the cell; it is selectively permeable (semi-permeable). It is a phospholipid bilayer: two layers of phospholipids with water-loving (hydrophilic) heads facing out and water-fearing (hydrophobic) tails facing in. Proteins are embedded in it (channels and pumps), plus cholesterol and carbohydrate tags. This is called the fluid mosaic model." },
{ t: "Diffusion and passive transport", k: "diffusion passive transport concentration gradient facilitated high low", x: "Passive transport moves substances across a membrane WITHOUT using energy (ATP). In diffusion, particles move from high concentration to low concentration until they are evenly spread (equilibrium). Small molecules like oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse straight through the membrane. Facilitated diffusion uses channel or carrier proteins to help bigger or charged particles (like glucose or ions) cross, still from high to low." },
{ t: "Osmosis and tonicity", k: "osmosis water hypertonic hypotonic isotonic swell shrink burst plasmolysis", x: "Osmosis is the diffusion of WATER across a selectively permeable membrane, from where there is more water (less solute) to where there is less water (more solute). Hypotonic solution (less solute outside): water moves in, the cell swells and an animal cell can burst. Hypertonic solution (more solute outside): water moves out and the cell shrinks (plasmolysis in plants). Isotonic: equal, no net change. Salt on a slug or salty water shrinking cells are hypertonic examples." },
{ t: "Active transport, endocytosis and exocytosis", k: "active transport pump atp sodium potassium endocytosis exocytosis phagocytosis against gradient", x: "Active transport uses energy (ATP) to move substances from LOW concentration to HIGH concentration, against the gradient, using protein pumps (example: the sodium-potassium pump). Endocytosis: the membrane wraps around something to bring it into the cell (phagocytosis = 'cell eating'). Exocytosis: vesicles fuse with the membrane to release things out of the cell." },
{ t: "Why cells are small", k: "cell size surface area volume ratio small divide", x: "Cells stay small because of the surface area to volume ratio. As a cell grows, its volume increases faster than its surface area, so the membrane can't move enough food, oxygen and waste in and out for the whole cell. Small cells have a high surface area to volume ratio, which makes exchange efficient. That is one reason cells divide." },
{ t: "Levels of organization", k: "levels organization cell tissue organ organ system organism multicellular specialized", x: "Levels of organization in multicellular organisms: cell -> tissue (similar cells working together, like muscle tissue) -> organ (different tissues working together, like the heart) -> organ system (organs working together, like the circulatory system) -> organism. Cells become specialized (differentiate) for different jobs; stem cells are unspecialized cells that can become many cell types." },
// ---------- biochemistry ----------
{ t: "Properties of water", k: "water polar hydrogen bond cohesion adhesion solvent specific heat ice", x: "Water is polar (slightly negative oxygen, slightly positive hydrogens), so water molecules form hydrogen bonds. This gives water: cohesion (sticks to itself, surface tension), adhesion (sticks to other things, capillary action in plants), high specific heat (resists temperature change), it's the 'universal solvent' (dissolves many substances), and ice is less dense than liquid water so it floats." },
{ t: "pH, acids and bases", k: "ph acid base neutral buffer hydrogen ions scale", x: "The pH scale goes from 0 to 14. pH 7 is neutral (pure water). Below 7 is acidic (more H+ ions, like lemon juice or stomach acid around pH 2). Above 7 is basic or alkaline (like bleach or soap). Each step is 10 times stronger. Buffers keep pH stable; human blood stays around pH 7.4." },
{ t: "Macromolecules (biomolecules)", k: "macromolecules biomolecules four organic molecules carbohydrates lipids proteins nucleic acids monomer polymer", x: "The four macromolecules of life are carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids. They are polymers built from smaller monomers: carbohydrates from monosaccharides (simple sugars), proteins from amino acids, nucleic acids from nucleotides; lipids are mostly made of fatty acids and glycerol. All contain carbon (organic). Joining monomers releases water (dehydration synthesis); breaking them uses water (hydrolysis)." },
{ t: "Carbohydrates", k: "carbohydrate sugar glucose starch cellulose glycogen monosaccharide polysaccharide quick energy", x: "Carbohydrates are made of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen (often in a 1:2:1 ratio). They give quick energy and structure. Monosaccharides are simple sugars like glucose (C6H12O6) and fructose. Disaccharides are two sugars (sucrose = table sugar). Polysaccharides are long chains: starch (energy storage in plants), glycogen (energy storage in animals, in liver and muscle) and cellulose (plant cell walls)." },
{ t: "Lipids", k: "lipid fat oil wax steroid phospholipid saturated unsaturated long term energy", x: "Lipids are fats, oils, waxes, phospholipids and steroids. They don't dissolve in water (hydrophobic). They store long-term energy (more than twice the energy per gram of carbohydrates), insulate the body, make up cell membranes (phospholipids) and act as hormones (steroids like testosterone; cholesterol). Saturated fats have no double bonds and are solid at room temperature; unsaturated fats have double bonds and are liquid (oils)." },
{ t: "Proteins", k: "protein amino acid peptide bond structure function denature", x: "Proteins are made of amino acids (20 kinds) linked by peptide bonds into chains that fold into a 3D shape. Their shape decides their job. Proteins do almost everything: enzymes speed up reactions, antibodies fight germs, hemoglobin carries oxygen, collagen and keratin give structure, actin and myosin move muscles, and some hormones (like insulin) are proteins. Heat or the wrong pH can denature (unfold) a protein so it stops working." },
{ t: "Nucleic acids", k: "nucleic acid dna rna nucleotide genetic information", x: "Nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) store and pass on genetic information. They are made of nucleotides, and each nucleotide has three parts: a sugar (deoxyribose in DNA, ribose in RNA), a phosphate group and a nitrogen base. ATP, the cell's energy molecule, is also a nucleotide." },
{ t: "Enzymes", k: "enzyme catalyst activation energy active site substrate lock key denature temperature", x: "Enzymes are proteins that act as catalysts: they speed up chemical reactions by lowering the activation energy, and they are not used up. Each enzyme has an active site shaped to fit one specific substrate (lock and key / induced fit). Enzymes work best at a certain temperature and pH; too much heat or the wrong pH denatures them (changes their shape) so they stop working. Enzyme names often end in -ase (lactase breaks down lactose)." },
{ t: "ATP", k: "atp adp adenosine triphosphate energy currency phosphate", x: "ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the energy 'currency' of the cell. It stores energy in the bonds between its three phosphate groups. When the cell breaks off one phosphate, ATP becomes ADP (adenosine diphosphate) and releases energy for work like muscle movement and active transport. Cellular respiration recharges ADP back into ATP." },
// ---------- energy ----------
{ t: "Photosynthesis", k: "photosynthesis equation reactants products glucose oxygen carbon dioxide sunlight plants autotroph", x: "Photosynthesis is how plants, algae and some bacteria turn light energy into chemical energy (sugar). It happens in chloroplasts. Equation: 6CO2 + 6H2O + light energy -> C6H12O6 + 6O2. Reactants: carbon dioxide, water and sunlight. Products: glucose (sugar) and oxygen. The oxygen is released into the air through stomata. It has two stages: the light-dependent reactions and the Calvin cycle." },
{ t: "Light reactions and Calvin cycle", k: "light dependent reactions calvin cycle light independent dark reactions thylakoid stroma nadph carbon fixation", x: "Stage 1, light-dependent reactions (in the thylakoid membranes): chlorophyll absorbs light, water is split, oxygen is released, and energy is stored in ATP and NADPH. Stage 2, the Calvin cycle or light-independent reactions (in the stroma): the ATP and NADPH are used to turn carbon dioxide into glucose (carbon fixation). It doesn't need light directly." },
{ t: "Cellular respiration", k: "cellular respiration equation aerobic glucose oxygen atp carbon dioxide water mitochondria", x: "Cellular respiration releases energy from food to make ATP. Almost all living things do it (plants too). Equation: C6H12O6 + 6O2 -> 6CO2 + 6H2O + ATP (energy). Reactants: glucose and oxygen; products: carbon dioxide, water and about 36-38 ATP. Three stages: glycolysis (cytoplasm), the Krebs cycle (mitochondria) and the electron transport chain (mitochondria inner membrane, makes the most ATP). It is the reverse of photosynthesis." },
{ t: "Stages of cellular respiration", k: "glycolysis krebs cycle citric acid electron transport chain pyruvate stages", x: "1) Glycolysis: in the cytoplasm, glucose splits into 2 pyruvate, making 2 ATP; it needs no oxygen. 2) Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle): in the mitochondria matrix, pyruvate is broken down, releasing CO2 and making 2 ATP plus NADH and FADH2. 3) Electron transport chain: on the inner mitochondrial membrane, uses NADH and FADH2 and oxygen (the final electron acceptor, forming water) to make about 32-34 ATP." },
{ t: "Fermentation (anaerobic respiration)", k: "fermentation anaerobic no oxygen lactic acid alcoholic yeast muscle soreness", x: "When there is no oxygen, cells use fermentation after glycolysis to keep making a little ATP (only 2 ATP per glucose). Lactic acid fermentation happens in your muscles during hard exercise (causes the burn) and in bacteria that make yogurt. Alcoholic fermentation happens in yeast and makes ethanol and carbon dioxide (makes bread rise and is used to make alcoholic drinks). Aerobic = with oxygen, anaerobic = without." },
{ t: "Photosynthesis vs cellular respiration", k: "compare photosynthesis respiration difference relationship opposite", x: "Photosynthesis: stores energy, happens in chloroplasts, only in producers (plants, algae), uses CO2 + H2O + light, makes glucose + O2. Cellular respiration: releases energy, happens in mitochondria, in almost all organisms (plants too), uses glucose + O2, makes CO2 + H2O + ATP. The products of one are the reactants of the other, so they form a cycle." },
{ t: "Autotrophs and heterotrophs", k: "autotroph heterotroph producer consumer make own food", x: "Autotrophs (producers) make their own food, usually by photosynthesis using sunlight (plants, algae, some bacteria) or by chemosynthesis using chemicals (bacteria at deep-sea vents). Heterotrophs (consumers) can't make their own food and must eat other organisms (animals, fungi, most bacteria)." },
// ---------- cell division ----------
{ t: "Cell cycle", k: "cell cycle interphase g1 s g2 mitotic phase checkpoint", x: "The cell cycle is the life of a cell. Interphase (most of the time): G1 (the cell grows and does its normal job), S (DNA is copied/replicated), G2 (more growth, getting ready to divide). Then the M phase: mitosis (the nucleus divides) and cytokinesis (the cytoplasm divides). Checkpoints stop the cycle if something is wrong, like damaged DNA." },
{ t: "Mitosis", k: "mitosis phases prophase metaphase anaphase telophase pmat identical daughter cells", x: "Mitosis divides the nucleus to make 2 identical daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent (diploid -> diploid). It is used for growth, repair and asexual reproduction. Phases (PMAT): Prophase - chromosomes condense and become visible, nuclear envelope breaks down, spindle forms. Metaphase - chromosomes line up in the Middle. Anaphase - sister chromatids are pulled Apart to opposite ends. Telophase - two new nuclei form. Then cytokinesis splits the cell." },
{ t: "Cytokinesis", k: "cytokinesis cleavage furrow cell plate divide cytoplasm", x: "Cytokinesis is the division of the cytoplasm after mitosis, making two separate cells. In animal cells, the membrane pinches in (cleavage furrow). In plant cells, a cell plate forms in the middle and becomes the new cell wall." },
{ t: "Meiosis", k: "meiosis gametes sex cells sperm egg haploid diploid four cells meiosis i ii", x: "Meiosis makes gametes (sex cells: sperm and eggs). One diploid cell (2n, 46 chromosomes in humans) divides TWICE (meiosis I and meiosis II) to make 4 haploid cells (n, 23 chromosomes), each genetically different. In meiosis I homologous chromosomes pair up and separate; in meiosis II sister chromatids separate. Fertilization (sperm + egg) brings the number back to diploid in the zygote." },
{ t: "Mitosis vs meiosis", k: "mitosis meiosis difference compare", x: "Mitosis: 1 division, makes 2 cells, identical to the parent, diploid (46 in humans), for growth and repair, in body (somatic) cells. Meiosis: 2 divisions, makes 4 cells, genetically different, haploid (23 in humans), for making gametes (sperm and egg), in sex organs, and includes crossing over." },
{ t: "Crossing over and genetic variation", k: "crossing over genetic variation independent assortment homologous chromosomes recombination", x: "Meiosis creates genetic variation in three ways: crossing over (homologous chromosomes swap pieces in prophase I), independent assortment (chromosome pairs line up and separate randomly), and random fertilization (any sperm can join any egg). This is why siblings (except identical twins) look different." },
{ t: "Chromosomes", k: "chromosome chromatid centromere homologous autosome sex chromosome karyotype 46 23 pairs xx xy", x: "Chromosomes are tightly coiled DNA. Humans have 46 chromosomes in body cells = 23 pairs: 22 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes (XX = female, XY = male). Homologous chromosomes are a matching pair, one from each parent. A copied chromosome is two sister chromatids joined at the centromere. A karyotype is a picture of all of a person's chromosomes in pairs." },
{ t: "Nondisjunction and chromosome disorders", k: "nondisjunction down syndrome trisomy 21 turner klinefelter extra chromosome", x: "Nondisjunction is when chromosomes fail to separate correctly during meiosis, so a gamete gets an extra or missing chromosome. Examples: Down syndrome (trisomy 21, three copies of chromosome 21), Turner syndrome (one X, XO), Klinefelter syndrome (XXY). These can be seen on a karyotype." },
{ t: "Cancer", k: "cancer tumor uncontrolled cell division mutation carcinogen benign malignant", x: "Cancer is uncontrolled cell division. It happens when mutations damage the genes that control the cell cycle, so cells ignore checkpoints and keep dividing, forming a tumor. Benign tumors stay in one place; malignant tumors invade tissue and can spread (metastasis). Carcinogens like UV light, tobacco smoke and some chemicals raise the risk." },
// ---------- genetics ----------
{ t: "Genetics vocabulary", k: "gene allele dominant recessive trait heredity mendel", x: "Heredity is passing traits from parents to offspring. A gene is a piece of DNA that codes for a trait. Alleles are different versions of a gene (T = tall, t = short). A dominant allele (capital letter) shows up whenever it is present. A recessive allele (lowercase) only shows if there are two copies. Gregor Mendel, the 'father of genetics', discovered these patterns with pea plants." },
{ t: "Genotype and phenotype", k: "genotype phenotype homozygous heterozygous purebred hybrid", x: "Genotype = the alleles an organism has (the letters, like TT, Tt or tt). Phenotype = the physical trait you can see (tall or short). Homozygous (purebred) = two of the same allele: TT (homozygous dominant) or tt (homozygous recessive). Heterozygous (hybrid) = two different alleles: Tt. Tt shows the dominant phenotype." },
{ t: "Punnett squares (monohybrid cross)", k: "punnett square cross probability ratio offspring tt monohybrid 3:1 heterozygous", x: "A Punnett square shows the possible offspring of a cross. Put one parent's alleles across the top and the other's down the side, then fill each box. Tt x Tt gives TT, Tt, Tt, tt: genotype ratio 1:2:1, phenotype ratio 3 dominant : 1 recessive (75% show the dominant trait, 25% recessive). Tt x tt gives Tt, Tt, tt, tt: 1:1, so 50% dominant, 50% recessive. TT x tt gives all Tt (100% dominant phenotype)." },
{ t: "Dihybrid cross", k: "dihybrid cross two traits 9:3:3:1 independent assortment", x: "A dihybrid cross follows two traits at once, using a 4x4 Punnett square (16 boxes). Each parent's gametes get one allele of each gene (AaBb makes AB, Ab, aB, ab). Crossing two double heterozygotes (AaBb x AaBb) gives a phenotype ratio of 9:3:3:1. This works because of the law of independent assortment." },
{ t: "Mendel's laws", k: "mendel law of segregation independent assortment dominance pea plants", x: "Mendel's laws: Law of dominance - one allele can hide another. Law of segregation - the two alleles for a gene separate when gametes form, so each gamete gets one allele. Law of independent assortment - genes for different traits are inherited separately (if they're on different chromosomes)." },
{ t: "Incomplete dominance and codominance", k: "incomplete dominance codominance blend pink roan red white flowers", x: "Incomplete dominance: neither allele is fully dominant, so heterozygotes show a blend (red x white snapdragons make pink flowers; RR red, RW pink, WW white). Codominance: both alleles show fully at the same time (a red and white roan cow, or AB blood type where both A and B show). Tip: incomplete = mixed, co = both show." },
{ t: "Blood types (multiple alleles)", k: "blood type abo a b ab o multiple alleles universal donor recipient rh", x: "ABO blood type has three alleles: IA, IB and i. IA and IB are codominant; i is recessive. Genotypes: Type A = IAIA or IAi, Type B = IBIB or IBi, Type AB = IAIB, Type O = ii. Type O negative is the universal donor; AB positive is the universal recipient. Rh factor (+ or -) is a separate gene." },
{ t: "Sex-linked traits", k: "sex linked x linked colorblindness hemophilia carrier males", x: "Sex-linked (X-linked) traits are carried on the X chromosome. Males (XY) have only one X, so one recessive allele makes them show the trait; females (XX) need two copies, and a female with one copy is a carrier. That is why colorblindness and hemophilia are more common in males. Written like XᴴXʰ (carrier female) and XʰY (affected male). A son gets his X from his mother." },
{ t: "Pedigrees", k: "pedigree family tree carrier circle square shaded", x: "A pedigree is a family tree that tracks a trait. Circles = females, squares = males. Shaded = has the trait, half-shaded (or dot) = carrier, a horizontal line connecting a circle and square = parents, vertical lines lead to children. If two unaffected parents have an affected child, the trait is recessive. If mostly males are affected, it may be X-linked." },
{ t: "Polygenic traits and environment", k: "polygenic traits height skin color many genes environment epigenetics", x: "Polygenic traits are controlled by many genes, so they show a range of phenotypes instead of just two (height, skin color, eye color). The environment can also affect traits: nutrition affects height, and temperature affects fur color in Himalayan rabbits and sex in some turtles." },
// ---------- molecular biology ----------
{ t: "DNA structure", k: "dna structure double helix nucleotide watson crick franklin sugar phosphate backbone bases", x: "DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is a double helix, like a twisted ladder. The sides are sugar (deoxyribose) and phosphate; the rungs are pairs of nitrogen bases held by hydrogen bonds. The four bases are adenine (A), thymine (T), guanine (G) and cytosine (C). Watson and Crick described the structure in 1953 using Rosalind Franklin's X-ray image (Photo 51)." },
{ t: "Base pairing rules", k: "base pairing complementary adenine thymine guanine cytosine uracil chargaff a-t g-c", x: "In DNA, A always pairs with T and G always pairs with C (Chargaff's rule: amount of A = T, G = C). So the complementary strand of ATG CCA is TAC GGT. In RNA there is no T; uracil (U) replaces it, so A pairs with U. Example: DNA TAC -> mRNA AUG." },
{ t: "DNA replication", k: "dna replication copy helicase polymerase semiconservative s phase", x: "DNA replication copies DNA before a cell divides (in the S phase). Helicase unzips the double helix by breaking the hydrogen bonds. DNA polymerase adds matching nucleotides to each old strand (A with T, G with C) and checks for mistakes. Ligase seals gaps. Each new DNA molecule has one old strand and one new strand, so it's called semiconservative." },
{ t: "RNA", k: "rna mrna trna rrna ribose uracil single strand difference dna", x: "RNA differs from DNA: it is single-stranded, has the sugar ribose, and uses uracil (U) instead of thymine (T). Three main types: mRNA (messenger) carries the gene's code from the nucleus to a ribosome; tRNA (transfer) brings amino acids to the ribosome and has an anticodon; rRNA (ribosomal) makes up ribosomes." },
{ t: "Protein synthesis overview (central dogma)", k: "protein synthesis central dogma gene expression dna rna protein", x: "The central dogma: DNA -> RNA -> protein. Step 1, transcription (in the nucleus): a gene's DNA is copied into mRNA. Step 2, translation (at a ribosome in the cytoplasm): the mRNA is read in codons and tRNA brings the matching amino acids, which are joined into a protein. The protein's shape determines the trait." },
{ t: "Transcription", k: "transcription mrna rna polymerase promoter nucleus copy", x: "Transcription happens in the nucleus. RNA polymerase attaches at a promoter, unwinds the DNA, and builds a strand of mRNA that is complementary to one DNA strand (with U instead of T: DNA A-T-G-C becomes mRNA U-A-C-G). In eukaryotes, introns are cut out and exons are joined (RNA splicing) before the mRNA leaves the nucleus." },
{ t: "Translation and codons", k: "translation codon anticodon amino acid start codon stop codon aug ribosome trna genetic code", x: "Translation happens at the ribosome. The mRNA is read three bases at a time; each triplet is a codon that codes for one amino acid (use a codon chart). It starts at the start codon AUG (methionine). tRNA molecules have an anticodon that matches the codon (codon AUG pairs with anticodon UAC) and carry the amino acid. The amino acids join with peptide bonds until a stop codon (UAA, UAG or UGA)." },
{ t: "Mutations", k: "mutation point substitution frameshift insertion deletion silent missense nonsense mutagen", x: "A mutation is a change in the DNA sequence. Point mutation (substitution): one base is swapped; it can be silent (same amino acid), missense (different amino acid, like sickle cell anemia) or nonsense (makes a stop codon). Frameshift mutations (insertion or deletion of a base) shift the whole reading frame and usually change every amino acid after it, so they are often worse. Mutagens like UV light, X-rays and chemicals cause mutations. Some mutations are harmful, some helpful, most have no effect." },
{ t: "Gene regulation", k: "gene expression regulation operon lac differentiation turned on off", x: "Every body cell has the same DNA, but cells only use (express) some genes, which is why nerve cells and skin cells differ (differentiation). Genes can be turned on or off. In bacteria, the lac operon turns on the genes to digest lactose only when lactose is present. In eukaryotes, transcription factors and how tightly DNA is packed control expression." },
{ t: "Biotechnology", k: "biotechnology genetic engineering gmo recombinant dna crispr pcr gel electrophoresis cloning dna fingerprinting", x: "Genetic engineering changes an organism's DNA. Recombinant DNA: a gene (like the human insulin gene) is cut with restriction enzymes and put into bacteria using a plasmid, so the bacteria make insulin. GMOs are genetically modified organisms (like pest-resistant corn). CRISPR is a tool that edits genes precisely. PCR makes millions of copies of DNA. Gel electrophoresis separates DNA pieces by size (small pieces move farthest) for DNA fingerprinting. Cloning makes a genetically identical copy (Dolly the sheep)." },
// ---------- evolution ----------
{ t: "Evolution and Darwin", k: "evolution darwin galapagos finches origin of species descent with modification theory", x: "Evolution is change in the inherited traits of a population over many generations. Charles Darwin, after studying finches and tortoises on the Galapagos Islands, proposed in 1859 (On the Origin of Species) that species change over time by natural selection and that all living things share common ancestors (descent with modification). Individuals don't evolve; populations do." },
{ t: "Natural selection", k: "natural selection survival of the fittest variation overproduction adaptation fitness", x: "Natural selection: 1) Variation - individuals in a population have different traits. 2) Inheritance - traits are passed on in genes. 3) Overproduction - more offspring are born than can survive, so they compete. 4) Selection - individuals with traits better suited to the environment survive and reproduce more ('survival of the fittest'; fitness = reproductive success). Over time the helpful traits become more common. Example: bacteria becoming resistant to antibiotics, peppered moths." },
{ t: "Adaptations", k: "adaptation camouflage mimicry structural behavioral physiological", x: "An adaptation is an inherited trait that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its environment. Structural: a physical feature (a cactus's spines, a polar bear's thick fur). Behavioral: something it does (birds migrating, bears hibernating). Physiological: how its body works (venom, a camel saving water). Camouflage helps blend in; mimicry is looking like a dangerous species." },
{ t: "Evidence for evolution", k: "evidence evolution fossils homologous analogous vestigial structures embryology dna comparative anatomy", x: "Evidence for evolution: Fossils show organisms changed over time (deeper rock layers are older). Homologous structures - same basic bones, different jobs, from a common ancestor (human arm, bat wing, whale flipper). Analogous structures - same job, different structure, NOT common ancestry (bird wing and insect wing). Vestigial structures - leftover parts with little use (human appendix, whale hip bones). Embryology - early embryos of vertebrates look similar. DNA and protein similarities show how closely species are related." },
{ t: "Types of selection", k: "stabilizing directional disruptive selection sexual artificial selection", x: "Stabilizing selection favors the average trait (human birth weight). Directional selection favors one extreme, shifting the average (giraffes with longer necks). Disruptive selection favors both extremes over the middle, which can split a population. Sexual selection favors traits that help attract mates (peacock tails). Artificial selection is when humans choose which organisms breed (dog breeds, crops)." },
{ t: "Speciation", k: "speciation species reproductive isolation geographic isolation allopatric new species", x: "A species is a group that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring. Speciation is the formation of new species. It usually happens through isolation: geographic isolation (a river or mountain splits a population, like the Kaibab and Abert squirrels at the Grand Canyon), behavioral isolation (different mating songs or dances) or temporal isolation (breeding at different times). Over time the separated groups become too different to interbreed." },
{ t: "Genetic drift, gene flow and Hardy-Weinberg", k: "genetic drift gene flow bottleneck founder effect hardy weinberg gene pool allele frequency", x: "A gene pool is all the alleles in a population. Evolution can also happen by: genetic drift - random changes in allele frequency, strongest in small populations (bottleneck effect after a disaster, founder effect when a few individuals start a new population); gene flow - alleles moving between populations by migration; and mutation. Hardy-Weinberg: allele frequencies stay the same if there's no mutation, no migration, a large population, random mating and no natural selection. Equations: p + q = 1 and p² + 2pq + q² = 1." },
{ t: "History of life and fossils", k: "fossil record geologic time scale extinction relative dating radiometric dating half life", x: "The fossil record shows life began as simple single cells about 3.5 billion years ago; Earth is about 4.6 billion years old. Relative dating uses rock layers (lower is older). Radiometric (absolute) dating uses the half-life of radioactive isotopes to find the actual age. Mass extinctions (like the dinosaurs 66 million years ago) were followed by new species filling empty niches." },
{ t: "Cladograms", k: "cladogram phylogenetic tree derived characteristics common ancestor branch", x: "A cladogram (phylogenetic tree) shows how species are related by shared derived characteristics. Each branch point (node) is a common ancestor. Species that share a more recent branch point are more closely related. Traits are added along the trunk, so organisms after a trait marker all have that trait." },
// ---------- classification & kingdoms ----------
{ t: "Taxonomy and classification", k: "taxonomy classification linnaeus levels domain kingdom phylum class order family genus species king philip", x: "Taxonomy is the science of naming and grouping organisms. Carl Linnaeus created the system. Levels from broadest to most specific: Domain, Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species (memory trick: 'Dear King Philip Came Over For Good Soup'). The more levels two organisms share, the more closely related they are." },
{ t: "Binomial nomenclature", k: "binomial nomenclature scientific name genus species homo sapiens latin", x: "Binomial nomenclature gives each species a two-part scientific name: Genus + species, in Latin, written in italics (or underlined), with only the Genus capitalized. Example: Homo sapiens (humans), Canis lupus (gray wolf). It avoids confusion from different common names." },
{ t: "Three domains and six kingdoms", k: "domains kingdoms bacteria archaea eukarya protista fungi plantae animalia eubacteria archaebacteria", x: "Three domains: Bacteria, Archaea (both prokaryotes) and Eukarya (eukaryotes). Six kingdoms: Eubacteria (common bacteria), Archaebacteria (live in extreme places like hot springs), Protista (mostly single-celled eukaryotes like amoeba, algae), Fungi (mushrooms, yeast, mold; absorb food, cell walls of chitin), Plantae (multicellular, photosynthesis, cellulose walls) and Animalia (multicellular, eat other organisms, no cell walls)." },
{ t: "Dichotomous keys", k: "dichotomous key identify organisms two choices", x: "A dichotomous key identifies an organism by a series of two-choice steps (1a or 1b). At each step, pick the description that fits, then go to the step it points to, until you reach a name. Each choice is about an observable feature, like 'has wings' vs 'has no wings'." },
{ t: "Viruses", k: "virus capsid host lytic lysogenic vaccine not living hiv flu antibiotic", x: "Viruses are tiny particles of DNA or RNA inside a protein coat (capsid). They are not cells, can't grow or make energy, and can only reproduce inside a host cell, so most scientists don't call them living. Lytic cycle: the virus takes over the cell, makes copies, and bursts the cell. Lysogenic cycle: the viral DNA hides in the host DNA for a while before becoming active. Antibiotics do NOT work on viruses; vaccines help prevent viral diseases (flu, measles, COVID-19, HIV causes AIDS)." },
{ t: "Bacteria", k: "bacteria prokaryote binary fission shapes cocci bacilli spirilla antibiotic resistance helpful", x: "Bacteria are single-celled prokaryotes. They reproduce by binary fission (splitting in two) and can share genes by conjugation. Shapes: cocci (round), bacilli (rods), spirilla (spirals). Most are helpful: they decompose dead material, fix nitrogen for plants, help digestion in your gut and make foods like yogurt. Some cause diseases (strep throat, food poisoning), which antibiotics can treat, but overusing antibiotics leads to resistant bacteria." },
{ t: "Protists", k: "protist amoeba paramecium euglena algae plasmodium malaria protozoa", x: "Protists are eukaryotes that aren't plants, animals or fungi, and most are single-celled. Animal-like protists (protozoa) like amoebas (move with pseudopods) and paramecia (cilia). Plant-like protists like algae and euglena do photosynthesis (algae make much of Earth's oxygen). Fungus-like protists include slime molds. Plasmodium is a protist that causes malaria, spread by mosquitoes." },
{ t: "Fungi", k: "fungi fungus mushroom yeast mold hyphae spores decomposer chitin", x: "Fungi (mushrooms, yeast, molds) are eukaryotes that are heterotrophs: they absorb nutrients from their surroundings, often as decomposers that break down dead things. They have cell walls made of chitin and bodies made of thread-like hyphae (a mass of hyphae is a mycelium). They reproduce with spores. Yeast is used for bread; penicillin (an antibiotic) comes from a mold." },
// ---------- plants ----------
{ t: "Plant structure: roots, stems, leaves", k: "plant parts roots stems leaves stomata guard cells function", x: "Roots anchor the plant and absorb water and minerals (root hairs increase surface area). Stems support the plant and carry water and food between roots and leaves. Leaves are the main site of photosynthesis; tiny pores called stomata (opened and closed by guard cells) let carbon dioxide in and oxygen and water vapor out." },
{ t: "Xylem, phloem and transpiration", k: "xylem phloem vascular tissue transpiration water transport sugar", x: "Vascular plants have two transport tissues. Xylem carries water and minerals UP from the roots to the leaves (one direction). Phloem carries sugars (food made by photosynthesis) from the leaves to the rest of the plant (both directions). Tip: 'xylem = water up, phloem = food flows'. Transpiration is water evaporating from the leaves through stomata, which pulls more water up the xylem (helped by cohesion and adhesion)." },
{ t: "Plant groups", k: "plant groups mosses ferns gymnosperms angiosperms vascular nonvascular seeds flowers", x: "Nonvascular plants (mosses) have no xylem or phloem, so they stay small and live in damp places; they use spores. Seedless vascular plants (ferns) have vascular tissue but use spores. Gymnosperms (conifers like pine trees) have seeds in cones, not in fruit. Angiosperms (flowering plants) have seeds inside fruit; they are the largest group. Monocots have one seed leaf and parallel veins; dicots have two seed leaves and branching veins." },
{ t: "Flowers and plant reproduction", k: "flower parts pollination stamen pistil anther filament stigma style ovary petal sepal fertilization seed fruit", x: "Flowers are the reproductive organs of angiosperms. Male part = stamen (anther makes pollen + filament). Female part = pistil or carpel (stigma catches pollen, style, ovary holds ovules). Petals attract pollinators; sepals protect the bud. Pollination moves pollen to the stigma (by wind, insects, birds). After fertilization, the ovule becomes a seed and the ovary becomes the fruit." },
{ t: "Plant responses (tropisms)", k: "tropism phototropism gravitropism geotropism thigmotropism auxin hormones", x: "Tropisms are plant growth responses to a stimulus. Phototropism: growing toward light (caused by the hormone auxin collecting on the shaded side, which makes those cells grow longer). Gravitropism (geotropism): roots grow down, stems grow up. Thigmotropism: responding to touch (vines wrapping around a pole)." },
// ---------- ecology ----------
{ t: "Ecology levels", k: "ecology organism population community ecosystem biome biosphere levels", x: "Ecology is the study of how organisms interact with each other and their environment. Levels: organism (one individual) -> population (one species in one area) -> community (all the different populations in an area) -> ecosystem (the community plus nonliving things) -> biome (large region with a similar climate) -> biosphere (all life on Earth)." },
{ t: "Biotic and abiotic factors", k: "biotic abiotic factors living nonliving", x: "Biotic factors are the living parts of an ecosystem (plants, animals, fungi, bacteria). Abiotic factors are the nonliving parts (sunlight, water, temperature, soil, air, rocks, pH). Both affect which organisms can live somewhere." },
{ t: "Food chains and food webs", k: "food chain food web producer consumer primary secondary tertiary herbivore carnivore omnivore decomposer arrows", x: "A food chain shows one path of energy: producer (plant) -> primary consumer (herbivore, eats plants) -> secondary consumer (eats herbivores) -> tertiary consumer. Arrows point in the direction energy flows, from the eaten to the eater. A food web is many connected food chains. Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) break down dead organisms and return nutrients to the soil. Herbivores eat plants, carnivores eat animals, omnivores eat both, scavengers eat dead animals." },
{ t: "Trophic levels and the 10% rule", k: "trophic level energy pyramid 10 percent rule biomass energy transfer", x: "Each step in a food chain is a trophic level. Only about 10% of the energy at one level passes to the next level; about 90% is used for life processes or lost as heat. Example: if plants have 10,000 kcal, primary consumers get about 1,000, secondary about 100, tertiary about 10. That's why energy pyramids get smaller at the top and why food chains rarely have more than 4-5 levels." },
{ t: "Symbiosis and species interactions", k: "symbiosis mutualism commensalism parasitism predation competition predator prey niche habitat", x: "Symbiosis is a close relationship between two species. Mutualism: both benefit (+/+), like bees and flowers. Commensalism: one benefits, the other is unaffected (+/0), like barnacles on a whale. Parasitism: one benefits and the host is harmed (+/-), like ticks or tapeworms. Other interactions: predation (predator eats prey) and competition (both want the same resources). A habitat is where an organism lives; a niche is its role (job) in the ecosystem." },
{ t: "Carbon cycle", k: "carbon cycle carbon dioxide photosynthesis respiration combustion fossil fuels decomposition", x: "Carbon moves between the air, living things, oceans and the ground. Photosynthesis takes CO2 out of the air and stores carbon in sugars. Cellular respiration and decomposition put CO2 back. Burning fossil fuels and forests (combustion) releases stored carbon as CO2, which adds to the greenhouse effect and climate change. Oceans also absorb lots of CO2." },
{ t: "Nitrogen cycle", k: "nitrogen cycle nitrogen fixation bacteria legumes nitrates denitrification ammonia", x: "Air is about 78% nitrogen gas, but plants and animals can't use it directly. Nitrogen-fixing bacteria (in soil and in root nodules of legumes like beans) turn nitrogen gas into ammonia and nitrates that plants absorb to make proteins and DNA. Animals get nitrogen by eating plants. Decomposers return nitrogen to the soil, and denitrifying bacteria turn nitrates back into nitrogen gas. Lightning also fixes some nitrogen." },
{ t: "Water cycle", k: "water cycle evaporation condensation precipitation transpiration runoff", x: "The water cycle moves water around Earth, powered by the sun. Evaporation: liquid water becomes vapor. Transpiration: plants release water vapor from leaves. Condensation: vapor cools into clouds. Precipitation: rain, snow, sleet or hail falls. Runoff and infiltration (soaking into groundwater) carry it back to oceans, lakes and rivers." },
{ t: "Population growth", k: "population growth exponential logistic carrying capacity limiting factors j curve s curve density dependent", x: "Exponential growth (J-shaped curve) happens when resources are unlimited and the population keeps doubling. Logistic growth (S-shaped curve) happens when limiting factors (food, water, space, disease, predators) slow growth until the population levels off at the carrying capacity, the largest population the environment can support. Density-dependent factors (disease, competition) get stronger as the population gets crowded; density-independent factors (weather, natural disasters) don't depend on population size." },
{ t: "Ecological succession", k: "succession primary secondary pioneer species lichens climax community", x: "Succession is the gradual change in a community over time. Primary succession starts on bare rock with no soil (after a volcano or glacier); pioneer species like lichens and mosses break down rock into soil first. Secondary succession happens where soil is still there after a disturbance (forest fire, abandoned farm) and is faster. It can lead to a stable climax community (like a mature forest)." },
{ t: "Biomes", k: "biome tundra desert rainforest grassland savanna taiga deciduous forest", x: "Biomes are large regions with similar climate and organisms. Tropical rainforest: hot, very wet, the most biodiversity. Desert: very dry, plants like cactus store water. Grassland/savanna: grasses, few trees, grazing animals. Temperate deciduous forest: four seasons, trees lose leaves in fall. Taiga (boreal forest): cold, conifers. Tundra: coldest, permafrost, no trees. Aquatic biomes: freshwater and marine (ocean)." },
{ t: "Biodiversity and human impact", k: "biodiversity human impact pollution habitat loss invasive species deforestation climate change endangered conservation keystone", x: "Biodiversity is the variety of life in an area; high biodiversity makes ecosystems more stable. Human threats: habitat destruction (deforestation), pollution, invasive species (non-native species with no predators that outcompete natives, like zebra mussels), overhunting/overfishing and climate change from greenhouse gases. A keystone species (like sea otters or wolves) has a huge effect on its ecosystem. Conservation includes protected areas, recycling and reducing fossil fuel use." },
// ---------- human body ----------
{ t: "Homeostasis and feedback", k: "homeostasis negative feedback positive feedback body temperature balance stable internal", x: "Homeostasis is keeping the body's internal conditions stable (temperature about 37 C / 98.6 F, blood sugar, water, pH). Most of it uses negative feedback, which reverses a change: if you get hot, you sweat to cool down; if you get cold, you shiver. Blood sugar: high -> insulin lowers it; low -> glucagon raises it. Positive feedback makes a change bigger until an event ends (contractions during childbirth, blood clotting)." },
{ t: "Digestive system", k: "digestive system mouth esophagus stomach small intestine large intestine liver pancreas villi bile enzymes digestion", x: "The digestive system breaks food into nutrients. Mouth: chewing (mechanical) and saliva with amylase starts digesting starch. Esophagus: moves food to the stomach by peristalsis (muscle waves). Stomach: acid and pepsin break down proteins. Small intestine: most digestion and absorption of nutrients into the blood through villi (finger-like folds). Liver makes bile (breaks up fats), stored in the gallbladder; pancreas makes enzymes. Large intestine: absorbs water, forms feces." },
{ t: "Circulatory system and the heart", k: "circulatory system heart chambers atrium ventricle arteries veins capillaries blood flow", x: "The circulatory system moves blood, carrying oxygen, nutrients and wastes. The human heart has 4 chambers: 2 atria (top, receive blood) and 2 ventricles (bottom, pump blood out). Right side pumps oxygen-poor blood to the lungs; left side pumps oxygen-rich blood to the body (the left ventricle has the thickest wall). Arteries carry blood Away from the heart (high pressure), veins carry it back (they have valves), and capillaries are tiny vessels where gases and nutrients are exchanged with cells." },
{ t: "Blood", k: "blood red blood cells white blood cells platelets plasma hemoglobin", x: "Blood has four parts. Plasma: the yellow liquid part (mostly water) carrying nutrients, hormones and wastes. Red blood cells: carry oxygen using hemoglobin (an iron protein); they have no nucleus. White blood cells: fight infection. Platelets: cell pieces that help blood clot. Blood cells are made in the bone marrow." },
{ t: "Respiratory system", k: "respiratory system lungs breathing alveoli trachea bronchi diaphragm gas exchange", x: "The respiratory system takes in oxygen and gets rid of carbon dioxide. Air path: nose/mouth -> pharynx -> larynx (voice box) -> trachea (windpipe) -> bronchi -> bronchioles -> alveoli. Alveoli are tiny air sacs surrounded by capillaries where gas exchange happens by diffusion: O2 goes into the blood, CO2 comes out. The diaphragm contracts (moves down) to pull air in (inhale) and relaxes to push it out (exhale)." },
{ t: "Nervous system", k: "nervous system brain spinal cord neurons cns pns cerebrum cerebellum brain stem reflex", x: "The nervous system controls the body with electrical signals. Central nervous system (CNS) = brain and spinal cord. Peripheral nervous system (PNS) = nerves going to the rest of the body. Brain parts: cerebrum (thinking, memory, senses, voluntary movement), cerebellum (balance and coordination), brain stem (automatic functions like breathing and heart rate). A reflex (like pulling your hand off a hot stove) goes through the spinal cord without waiting for the brain." },
{ t: "Neurons", k: "neuron nerve cell dendrite axon synapse neurotransmitter impulse myelin", x: "Neurons are nerve cells that carry signals (impulses). Dendrites receive signals, the cell body has the nucleus, and the axon carries the signal away, often covered by a myelin sheath that speeds it up. At the end, the signal crosses a small gap called a synapse using chemicals called neurotransmitters (like dopamine and serotonin). Sensory neurons carry info to the CNS, motor neurons carry commands to muscles, and interneurons connect them." },
{ t: "Endocrine system", k: "endocrine system hormones glands pituitary thyroid adrenal pancreas insulin", x: "The endocrine system uses hormones, chemical messengers carried in the blood, to control growth, metabolism and other slower, longer-lasting processes. Glands: pituitary ('master gland', growth hormone), thyroid (metabolism), adrenal (adrenaline for fight-or-flight), pancreas (insulin and glucagon control blood sugar), ovaries (estrogen) and testes (testosterone). Diabetes happens when the body doesn't make or respond to insulin properly." },
{ t: "Immune system", k: "immune system pathogen antibodies antigens white blood cells vaccine immunity b cells t cells innate", x: "The immune system defends against pathogens (disease-causing germs: bacteria, viruses, fungi, parasites). First line: skin, mucus, stomach acid. Second line (innate, non-specific): inflammation, fever and white blood cells (phagocytes) that eat germs. Third line (specific/adaptive): B cells make antibodies that lock onto antigens (markers on germs), and T cells kill infected cells. Memory cells remember germs for faster future responses. Vaccines contain weakened, dead or pieces of a pathogen to build that memory without getting sick." },
{ t: "Excretory system", k: "excretory urinary system kidneys nephron urine bladder waste urea", x: "The excretory system removes wastes. The kidneys filter blood through millions of tiny units called nephrons, removing urea, extra water and salts to make urine, and returning useful things to the blood. Urine goes through the ureters to the bladder and leaves through the urethra. Lungs (CO2), skin (sweat) and liver (breaks down toxins, makes urea) also help remove wastes." },
{ t: "Skeletal and muscular systems", k: "skeletal system bones muscular system muscles joints ligaments tendons skeletal smooth cardiac", x: "The skeletal system (206 bones in adults) supports and protects the body, lets it move, stores calcium, and makes blood cells in the marrow. Ligaments connect bone to bone; tendons connect muscle to bone. Three muscle types: skeletal (voluntary, moves bones, striped), smooth (involuntary, in organs like the stomach and blood vessels) and cardiac (involuntary, only in the heart). Muscles work in pairs and can only pull (contract), never push, like the biceps and triceps." },
{ t: "Reproductive system and development", k: "reproductive system fertilization zygote embryo fetus sperm egg ovary testes uterus pregnancy", x: "Males make sperm in the testes; females make eggs (ova) in the ovaries. Fertilization (usually in the fallopian tube) joins a sperm and egg (each haploid, 23 chromosomes) into a diploid zygote (46). The zygote divides by mitosis into an embryo, which implants in the uterus and develops into a fetus, getting nutrients and oxygen through the placenta and umbilical cord. Pregnancy lasts about 9 months (40 weeks)." },
{ t: "Integumentary system (skin)", k: "integumentary skin hair nails epidermis dermis melanin sweat", x: "The integumentary system is the skin, hair and nails. Skin is the largest organ. It protects against germs and water loss, controls temperature (sweat glands, blood vessels), senses touch and makes vitamin D in sunlight. The epidermis is the outer layer (has melanin, the pigment that protects from UV); the dermis below has nerves, blood vessels, hair follicles and glands." },
{ t: "Nutrition", k: "nutrition nutrients vitamins minerals calories diet carbohydrates protein fat fiber", x: "The six nutrient groups are carbohydrates (main energy source), proteins (building and repairing tissue), fats (stored energy, insulation), vitamins (help reactions; vitamin C prevents scurvy, vitamin D helps bones), minerals (calcium for bones, iron for blood) and water (most important; the body is about 60% water). Energy in food is measured in Calories. Fiber helps digestion." },
]);
